### TASK 2 — Target lock engine
**Goal:** Cursor stays on target element through scroll, resize, route change

Build:
1. Create src/overlay/TargetLock.ts
2. TargetLock takes a target element reference and a callback 
   onBoundsChanged(rect: DOMRect).
3. Use ResizeObserver on the target element.
4. Use IntersectionObserver to detect when target scrolls out of view 
   — when out of view, emit onTargetLost().
5. On scroll events (window + all scrollable ancestors up the DOM tree): 
   recompute getBoundingClientRect() and call onBoundsChanged.
6. On window resize: recompute and call onBoundsChanged.
7. On route change (History API patch — pushState, replaceState, popstate): 
   attempt to re-find the target by its stable ID from ElementRegistry. 
   If found: re-lock. If not found after 2000ms: emit onTargetLost().
8. Expose: lock(element), unlock(), isLocked(), currentRect().

Integrate into ElementHighlighter.ts:
- When a point target is set, call TargetLock.lock(element)
- On onBoundsChanged: move highlight rect via transform (not left/top)
- On onTargetLost: show "lost target" visual state on highlight, 
  emit event to ClickyClient so it can re-ask AI for new target

Files: create src/overlay/TargetLock.ts, modify src/overlay/ElementHighlighter.ts,
       modify src/core/ClickyClient.ts (handle onTargetLost event)

Acceptance criteria:
- Highlight follows element while user scrolls the page
- Highlight updates when viewport resizes
- On route change, highlight re-attaches or fires onTargetLost
- Drift under 4 CSS pixels during scroll at 60fps

